This file is a capture of a couple of messages which cover the topic of passing
your Environment Variables from an OS/2 session over to a DOS session.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Date: 07-28-95 (11:30)              Number: 7177 of 7499 (Refer# NONE)
  To: IVAN WINDON
From: DAVID TERRY
Subj: Beta PCBOARD.EXE features
Read: 07-29-95 (10:43)                       Status: PUBLIC MESSAGE
Conf: Beta Support (5)                    Read Type: TEXT SCAN (+) HAS REPLIES

-> Also just a little problem, but everything but one door works on the new
-> OS/2 code, it's the Tradewars door, it's not taking the SET TWNODE=1 anymore
-> in the board.cmd file.

The problem is that the DOS ENVIRONMENT does not know what you put into your
OS/2 ENVIRONMENT.

So when you put the SET TWNODE=1 into your OS/2 environment, that setting is
*not* available to programs running inside of a DOS session.

However, there may be an easy solution for you.  Is this TWNODE=1 supposed to
be defining your PCBoard node number (but for TradeWars' usage)?  If so, then
try putting this line inside of the TradeWars door file itself:

        SET TWNODE=%PCBNODE%

That way the TWNODE environment variable will be set at the time that TradeWars
is run, and it will pull the proper node number from PCBoard's environment
variable.

        David W. Terry
   Clark Development Company
