Date: 08-03-95 (06:16)              Number: 7402 of 7499 (Refer# NONE)
  To: GREG HETMAN
From: DAVID TERRY
Subj: Environmental Variables
Read: 08-03-95 (09:20)                       Status: PUBLIC MESSAGE
Conf: Beta Support (5)                    Read Type: TEXT SCAN (+) HAS REPLIES

-> For running Doors under PCB 4 OS/2, I am still using standard Batchfiles for
-> running the doors (although modified for the CALL statement in BOARD.CMD).
-> I was under the impression that the Environmental variables are
-> automatically passed to the DOS window as soon as the shell window is
-> created.  This is a DOS window correct?

Normally, when OS/2 creates a DOS window it does NOT pass *any* environment
variables between the parent OS/2 window and the child DOS window.  None
whatsoever.

However, because PCBoard is control of creating this "DOS Window" in which the
DOOR application is run, PCBoard will pass any environment variables that it
*knows* about, over to the DOS window.  These environment variables are the
PCBxxxx= variables (i.e. PCB=, PCBDIR=, PCBDAT=, PCBDRIVE=, etc).  But PCBoard
also realizes that there is a DSZPORT= and DSZLOG= environment variable that is
likely to be set, so it passes these through too (if they happen to exist).

But PCBoard does NOT attempt to pass through any other environment variables.
In other words, if you edit your BOARD.CMD file and put a line in there that
says SET MYVARIABLE=XYZ.  That environment setting will go into the OS/2
session in which BOARD.CMD is running, but it will NOT be passed through to the
DOS session because OS/2 doesn't pass it, and because PCBoard doesn't know
about it so PCBoard doesn't pass it either.  So the environment variable is
useful to any OS/2 session windows that PCBoard spawns off, but any DOS windows
it spawns will NOT see this environment variable.

There are ways around this though...  you could have your BOARD.CMD file (or
STARTOS2.CMD if you're using it) create a .BAT file.  For example, you might
use the line "ECHO SET MYVARIABLE=XYZ > MYSETS.BAT" in your CMD file, then in
your DOS batch file you would put a "CALL MYSETS.BAT" file to run this BAT file
and pull the set statement(s) into your DOS environment.

In fact, this is what PCBoard is having to do.  It is creating a PCBDOS.BAT
file which is where it puts all of the environment variables that *it* knows
about.  Then whenever it runs a DOS program, it actually calls PCBDOS.BAT
*first* so that the SET statements can get executed.  PCBoard creates this file
every time it recycles, however, so you can't just modify this batch file to
suit your needs because any modifications you make to it will be lost.

One more idea for you.  If the environment variable you need could be "derived"
from the information already in the environment, then your DOOR batch file
could be the one to create the SET statement.

For example, there is a PCBPORT= environment variable which holds the port
number of the comm port.  If your door simply needs to know what the port
number is, but it uses a MYPORT= environment, then your door batch file could
have a line in it that says "SET MYPORT=%PCBPORT%" and in that manner, the door
batch file itself would create the MYPORT= environment variable each time it is
run.

Give those ideas some thought.  The work arounds aren't difficult, but knowing
that a workaround is required because environment variables aren't passed
between OS/2 and DOS sessions is a necessary first step.

        David W. Terry
   Clark Development Company
